Guttate Psoriasis (Mild Post-Infectious)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Small teardrop-shaped papules on the trunk, transient nature

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-019
Reason for Visit: Acute eruption of a widespread rash requiring exclusion of a systemic drug reaction or severe infectious exanthem.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a sudden, rapidly spreading rash over the torso. Immediate clinical review was necessary to differentiate a benign autoimmune flare from an acute anaphylactic drug reaction, meningococcemia, or a highly contagious exanthem prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I woke up covered in these red dots everywhere. I had a sore throat last week, and now I'm worried I'm having an allergic reaction to the antibiotics.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports completing a course of oral penicillin for streptococcal pharyngitis three days ago. Tonight, the patient noticed a sudden eruption of a rash spreading across the abdomen and back. The rapid onset triggered intense fear of a severe drug allergy. The rash is mildly itchy but not painful. Patient denies lip swelling, shortness of breath, wheeze, or current fever.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 82 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, no signs of respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals hundreds of small teardrop-shaped papules on the trunk, transient nature, with fine overlying silvery scale. Lesions are erythematous but blanch under pressure (glass test negative). No mucosal involvement, no conjunctivitis, no facial angioedema. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are normal. Resolving cervical lymphadenopathy noted from recent pharyngitis. Self-limiting drop-like lesions post-streptococcal are clinically evident.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment, no new environmental exposures or detergents identified.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Finished penicillin course; no other medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Guttate Psoriasis (Mild Post-Infectious) - Status: Acute Onset
Differentials:

Anaphylactic Drug Eruption - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of urticarial wheals, normal hemodynamics, no respiratory distress, and no mucosal swelling.

Meningococcal Septicemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile, well appearance, and completely blanching nature of the rash (negative glass test).

Pityriasis Rosea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a herald patch and the classic post-streptococcal timeline strongly favoring guttate psoriasis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a sudden, widespread truncal exanthem following antibiotic use, requiring immediate exclusion of a life-threatening anaphylactic reaction or meningococcemia. Triage correctly mandated urgent evaluation to ensure airway stability and hemodynamic safety. Clinical examination confirmed the airway was secure and the rash was a blanching, classic post-streptococcal autoimmune reaction (guttate psoriasis) rather than an acute infectious or allergic emergency. This bedside diagnosis safely prevented an emergency department presentation for suspected anaphylaxis and could not have been deferred due to the theoretical risk of airway compromise if it had been a true allergy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the benign, non-allergic nature of the rash. Prescribed mild topical corticosteroid and recommended liberal emollient use.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a thick, unscented moisturizer to the affected areas within 3 minutes of exiting the shower. By trapping moisture in the skin immediately, you will rapidly reduce the scaling and itch, making the rash much more comfortable to manage over the coming weeks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 1-2 weeks for ongoing dermatological management or possible phototherapy referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop any difficulty breathing, swelling of your lips or tongue, or if the rash becomes non-blanching (does not fade when you press a glass against it) with a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on clinical presentation; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This rash is your immune system overreacting to the recent throat infection. It is not an allergy. It will likely take several weeks to fade completely but is harmless.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical attention if the spots become filled with pus, or if your joints suddenly become severely swollen and painful.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The recent strep throat triggered a temporary skin inflammation. It is not contagious. Regular moisturizers and mild steroid creams will help it settle.
Actionable Steps: Avoid hot showers, which will dry the skin and worsen the itch. Use copious amounts of plain moisturizer daily.

